+# Block placement performance review
+
+Review of six suggested optimizations for `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau`
+(server) and `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau` (client).
+
+## The load we're dealing with
+
+- The server rejects placements closer together than `MIN_INTERVAL = 0.1` s, so each player can
+  place at most 10 blocks a second.
+- Servers hold 8 players, so the server handles **at most 80 PlaceBlock events a second**, and
+  each client fires at most around 10 a second while it is clicking or tapping.
+- Nobody has reported lag.
+
+80 events a second is very little work. Each handler runs some type checks, a hash lookup, a
+distance check, and creates one Part. That adds up to microseconds per event, well under 1% of a
+single frame. None of these suggestions could make a difference a player would notice. The real
+question for each one is whether it costs anything in correctness or complexity.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts instead of calling `Instance.new`: **No**
+
+A pool helps when objects are destroyed and created over and over (projectiles, effects), because
+you reuse the destroyed ones. This code never destroys a block. Every placed block stays in the
+world, so nothing would ever go back into the pool. A pool would just mean creating the Parts
+early instead of when they're needed. With at most 80 Parts a second, `Instance.new` costs
+nothing worth saving, and most of the real cost of a block (replication, physics, rendering)
+happens however the Part was made. If we add block removal later and profiling shows Part
+creation matters, we can revisit this.
+
+### 2. Pack PlaceBlock's arguments into a buffer: **No**
+
+Each event sends a short string (at most 6 characters) and a Vector3, which is a few dozen bytes
+including overhead. At the 80 events/s server-wide maximum that's only a few KB/s. A single
+replicated Part costs more bandwidth than the remote call that asked for it. A buffer would save
+a handful of bytes per event, and in exchange we'd have to write encoding code on the client and
+decoding code on the server. The server would then have to check buffer lengths and offsets on
+input an exploiter controls, and those checks are easy to get wrong. That buys us nothing here.
+
+### 3. Make PlaceBlock an `UnreliableRemoteEvent`: **No, this would break things**
+
+Unreliable events aren't faster at getting through. They skip retransmission and ordering, so
+some packets **get dropped** and the rest can arrive out of order. They're meant for frequent,
+replaceable data where only the newest value matters, like cursor positions or cosmetic effects.
+A block placement is a one-off action the player expects to happen. Dropping it means a click
+quietly does nothing. A dropped or reordered placement also fights with the server's 0.1 s rate
+limit and the occupied-cell check.
+
+It would also break the client code as written. `BlockPlacer.start` asserts
+`remote:IsA("RemoteEvent")`, and `UnreliableRemoteEvent` is not a `RemoteEvent` subclass (both
+inherit from `BaseRemoteEvent`), so the client would error on start.
+
+### 4. Add `--!native` to both scripts: **No**
+
+Native code generation speeds up Luau that does heavy computation in tight loops: math, buffer
+work, long-running algorithms. These scripts spend almost all their time in engine API calls
+(`Instance.new`, setting properties, `Raycast`, `FireServer`), and native codegen doesn't make
+those any faster. Both handlers run at most a few dozen times a second, so even a big speedup on
+the Luau part would be lost in the noise. Native code also uses more memory, and the engine
+limits how much native code an experience can have, so it's better saved for code that profiling
+shows is hot.
+
+### 5. Replace the `table.find` check with a lookup table: **Done, for clarity, not speed**
+
+`table.find` searched a 5-item list, which takes nanoseconds, so speed doesn't matter here.
+But a lookup table already existed: `BlockTypes.ByName`. The server built a separate
+`VALID_NAMES` list just to validate the name and then looked the name up in `ByName` anyway.
+That meant two sources of truth for the same check. The handler now does one lookup and rejects
+the request if it comes back `nil`:
+
+```lua
+local blockType = BlockTypes.ByName[blockTypeName]
+if not blockType then
+	return
+end
+```
+
+The behavior is the same, since both were built from `BlockTypes.List` by `Name`, and
+`VALID_NAMES` is gone. I made this change because it simplifies the code, not because of
+performance.
+
+(The client's `table.find(NUMBER_KEYS, input.KeyCode)` runs over a 5-item list once per key
+press. That's also negligible, so I left it alone.)
+
+### 6. Create the `RaycastParams` once instead of on every click: **No**
+
+Making a `RaycastParams` on each click costs almost nothing, and clicks happen at human speed.
+Reusing one object isn't free, either. The filter has to exclude the *current* character, which
+changes every time the player respawns. A shared `RaycastParams` would mean either reassigning
+`FilterDescendantsInstances` on every click anyway, which copies the array just like now, or
+keeping it up to date with `CharacterAdded`, which adds a stale-filter bug for no measurable gain.
+The current code always uses the right character, and that's worth more.
+
+## Summary
+
+| # | Suggestion | Verdict |
+|---|------------|---------|
+| 1 | Pool Parts | No: blocks are never destroyed, so nothing would be reused |
+| 2 | Buffer-pack arguments | No: saves a few bytes and adds risky decoding code |
+| 3 | UnreliableRemoteEvent | No: placements could be dropped, and the client assert would fail |
+| 4 | `--!native` | No: the time goes to engine API calls, which native code doesn't speed up |
+| 5 | Lookup table instead of `table.find` | Done, to simplify (reuses `BlockTypes.ByName`) |
+| 6 | Reuse `RaycastParams` | No: no measurable gain, and the filter can go stale on respawn |
+
+If lag shows up later, start by profiling with the MicroProfiler and the Developer Console's
+Network/Memory tabs. At this scale, the number and complexity of placed Parts (physics,
+rendering, replication to joining players) will matter much more than how the placement
+handler is written.
